1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,048
Hi, let me show you TaulSaathi.

2
00:00:02,208 --> 00:00:06,773
It’s a Kannada and English prototype for recording a small scrap
transaction.

3
00:00:06,933 --> 00:00:13,547
The problem is simple: if fifteen is heard as fifty, the
multiplication can be correct while the receipt is wrong.

4
00:00:13,707 --> 00:00:16,971
So this app makes the numbers visible before you confirm them.

5
00:00:17,131 --> 00:00:18,709
Here is the capture panel.

6
00:00:18,869 --> 00:00:23,563
You can type a supported transaction or upload a short WAV
recording.

7
00:00:23,723 --> 00:00:26,091
Gnani Prisma handles speech to text.

8
00:00:26,251 --> 00:00:31,691
This saved example used generated Kannada speech and an actual API
request.

9
00:00:31,851 --> 00:00:34,987
It’s a fictional transaction, not a customer recording.

10
00:00:35,147 --> 00:00:38,709
The paper weighs twenty-five kilograms including its container.

11
00:00:38,869 --> 00:00:40,747
The container weighs two kilograms.

12
00:00:40,907 --> 00:00:46,965
That leaves twenty-three kilograms at twenty rupees per kilogram,
giving four hundred and sixty rupees.

13
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:52,373
The app keeps the transcript alongside the fields, so I can inspect
where each value came from.

14
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:56,096
Gnani Timbre reads the critical values back in Kannada.

15
00:00:56,256 --> 00:00:58,603
Here is the audio returned for this record.

16
00:01:13,163 --> 00:01:16,981
Now I’ll switch to a separate typed example with conflicting rates.

17
00:01:17,141 --> 00:01:19,893
Both fifteen and fifty appear in the transcript.

18
00:01:20,053 --> 00:01:23,360
The app keeps that conflict visible and blocks confirmation.

19
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:26,357
It does not silently guess which price was intended.

20
00:01:26,517 --> 00:01:29,973
I need to check the agreement and enter an explicit correction.

21
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:34,955
For this fictional example, I choose fifteen rupees per kilogram and
record why.

22
00:01:35,115 --> 00:01:37,632
Applying the correction creates a new revision.

23
00:01:37,792 --> 00:01:40,075
The transcript warning still needs review.

24
00:01:40,235 --> 00:01:47,573
Once I’ve checked the full transcript and the calculated amount, I
can confirm that revision and download its JSON record.

25
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:51,168
Watch what happens if I change the rate after confirmation.

26
00:01:51,328 --> 00:01:54,997
The amount updates, but the old confirmation is invalidated.

27
00:01:55,157 --> 00:01:57,525
The changed record needs a fresh review.

28
00:01:57,685 --> 00:02:03,531
The correction history preserves what changed; an earlier approval
cannot authorize a different amount.

29
00:02:03,691 --> 00:02:09,109
The prototype uses Python, a small browser interface, and Gnani
speech APIs.

30
00:02:09,269 --> 00:02:12,149
Decimal arithmetic handles the calculation.

31
00:02:12,309 --> 00:02:17,664
The documented version passed one hundred and ten offline tests and
fifty text cases.

32
00:02:17,824 --> 00:02:21,024
Those are software checks, not speech accuracy scores.

33
00:02:21,184 --> 00:02:26,560
Human Kannada recordings and buyer or seller feedback are still the
next validation step.

34
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:29,835
That’s TaulSaathi: say it, check it, and keep a record.

35
00:02:29,995 --> 00:02:32,811
The source and evidence are available on GitHub.

36
00:02:32,971 --> 00:02:34,251
Thanks for watching.
